 ## Overview
-Shadow Receiving for Volumes is a technique that allows volumetric data to receive shadows cast by polygonal geometry, enhancing depth perception and realism without requiring the volume to be rendered twice [S1]. Unlike standard shadow pipelines that render shadow-casting objects separately, this method integrates shadow occlusion checks directly into the volume ray traversal process, significantly reducing computational overhead [S1].
+Shadow Receiving for Volumes is a technique that allows volumetric data to receive shadows cast by polygonal actors without requiring the volume to be rendered twice [S1]. It solves the performance bottleneck associated with traditional shadow pipelines, where rendering a volume as a shadow caster or receiver typically incurs significant overhead due to double traversal [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that compute self-shadowing or light-space transmittance buffers, this method integrates shadow occlusion checks directly into the view-space ray traversal against a pre-computed shadow map [S1]. It is distinct from [Lookup Table Shadow Rendering](3588-lookup-table-shadow-rendering.md) and [Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping](1415-ray-marching-with-shadow-mapping.md) by relying on standard shadow map textures rather than 3D transmittance textures or light-strength buffers [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates within a GPU-based volume ray casting pipeline, specifically utilizing the `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper2` architecture [S1]. It avoids the performance penalty of double rendering by checking shadow occlusion against a pre-computed shadow map during the traversal of each view ray [S1].
+The method operates within a GPU-based volume ray casting pipeline (specifically `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper2` in VTK) [S1]. It utilizes a shadow map generated from the light's perspective, which contains depth information for polygonal actors [S1]. During the volume rendering pass, the ray caster samples this shadow map to determine if the current ray segment is occluded by any polygon [S1].
 
-### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs**:
